        "number": 2,
        "title": "HTML From Zero",
        "subtitle": "Semantic structure, modern document architecture, typography, forms, and web accessibility",
        "readingTime": "25 min",
        "summary": "Master the foundational language of the web: semantic HTML5 tags, document anatomy, accessible media, tables, and validated user input forms.",
        "content": "### Learning Objectives\nBy the end of this module, you will be able to:\n1. Construct valid HTML5 documents using proper DOCTYPE, head metadata, and body elements.\n2. Structure page content with modern Semantic HTML elements (`<header>`, `<nav>`, `<main>`, `<article>`, `<section>`, `<footer>`).\n3. Build robust forms with accessible labels, input validation constraints, and accessible button triggers.\n4. Embed responsive, accessible images and media using the `<picture>`, `<figure>`, and `alt` attributes.\n5. Apply fundamental WCAG accessibility principles (A11y) to make websites usable for people with disabilities.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Anatomy of an HTML5 Document\n\nEvery modern webpage begins with a standard HTML5 skeleton:\n\n```html\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n  <head>\n    <meta charset=\"UTF-8\" />\n    <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\" />\n    <title>Professional Profile | FAHMRISE</title>\n    <meta name=\"description\" content=\"Frontend developer portfolio and project showcase.\" />\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <!-- Visible content rendered here -->\n    <h1>Welcome to My Frontend Journey</h1>\n    <p>Building accessible, performant websites from scratch.</p>\n  </body>\n</html>\n```\n\n#### Key Metadata Tags:\n- `<!DOCTYPE html>`: Enforces HTML5 rendering mode in all modern browsers.\n- `<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">`: Guarantees support for universal characters, symbols, and languages.\n- `<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\">`: Enables responsive mobile scaling.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Semantic HTML vs. Non-Semantic Divs\n\nSemantic tags describe the **meaning** of the content to browsers, search engines, and screen readers:\n\n```\n+-----------------------------------------------------------+\n|                        <header>                           |\n|  <nav> [Home] [Courses] [Projects] [Contact] </nav>       |\n+-----------------------------------------------------------+\n|                          <main>                           |\n|  +-----------------------------+  +--------------------+  |\n|  |          <article>          |  |      <aside>       |  |\n|  |  <h1>Article Title</h1>     |  | Related Resources  |  |\n|  |  <section>Section 1</section|  | Quick Links        |  |\n|  +-----------------------------+  +--------------------+  |\n+-----------------------------------------------------------+\n|                        <footer>                           |\n|         © 2026 FAHMRISE • All Rights Reserved             |\n+-----------------------------------------------------------+\n```\n\n#### Why Semantic HTML is Crucial:\n1. **Accessibility**: Screen readers allow visually impaired users to jump directly between `<main>`, `<nav>`, and `<header>` landmarks.\n2. **SEO**: Search engine crawlers prioritize content inside semantic tags.\n3. **Maintainability**: Clean, readable markup that any developer can understand instantly.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Accessible Forms & User Input\n\nForms are how users submit data. Every input **must** have an associated `<label>`:\n\n```html\n<form action=\"/api/contact\" method=\"POST\" class=\"contact-form\">\n  <div class=\"form-group\">\n    <label for=\"user-name\">Full Name (Required)</label>\n    <input \n      type=\"text\" \n      id=\"user-name\" \n      name=\"fullName\" \n      placeholder=\"e.g. Sarah Kamau\" \n      required \n      minlength=\"2\"\n    />\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"form-group\">\n    <label for=\"user-email\">Email Address</label>\n    <input \n      type=\"email\" \n      id=\"user-email\" \n      name=\"email\" \n      placeholder=\"sarah@example.com\" \n      required \n    />\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"form-group\">\n    <label for=\"track-select\">Choose Learning Track</label>\n    <select id=\"track-select\" name=\"track\">\n      <option value=\"frontend\">Frontend Developer</option>\n      <option value=\"data\">Data Analysis</option>\n      <option value=\"cyber\">Cybersecurity</option>\n    </select>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"form-group\">\n    <label for=\"user-message\">Your Goals</label>\n    <textarea id=\"user-message\" name=\"message\" rows=\"4\" placeholder=\"Tell us about your learning goals...\"></textarea>\n  </div>\n\n  <button type=\"submit\">Submit Application</button>\n</form>\n```\n\n---\n\n### 4. Accessible Media & Images\n\n```html\n<!-- Standard Accessible Image -->\n<img \n  src=\"/images/laptop-code.jpg\" \n  alt=\"Student writing JavaScript code in VS Code on a laptop\" \n  width=\"800\" \n  height=\"500\" \n  loading=\"lazy\"\n/>\n\n<!-- Semantic Figure with Caption -->\n<figure>\n  <img src=\"/images/box-model.png\" alt=\"Diagram showing margin, border, padding, and content\" />\n  <figcaption>Figure 1.1: The CSS Box Model hierarchy.</figcaption>\n</figure>\n```\n\n---\n\n### 5. Activities & Practical Exercises\n\n#### Exercise 1: Semantic Portfolio Skeleton\nCreate an `index.html` file for a personal developer portfolio containing:\n1. A valid HTML5 doctype and viewport meta tag.\n2. A `<header>` with a site title and a semantic `<nav>` with 3 links.\n3. A `<main>` section with an `<article>` featuring your bio and an ordered list of 3 web skills.\n4. A contact form with labels, inputs for name, email, and a submit button.\n5. A `<footer>` with copyright information.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Module Knowledge Quiz\n\n**Question 1:** Why is pairing a `<label>` with an `<input>` via the `for` and `id` attributes essential?\n- A) It automatically sets the CSS text color to green\n- B) It allows screen readers to announce the input's purpose and expands the clickable tap target for mobile users *(Correct)*\n- C) It sends the form data twice for redundancy\n- D) It encrypts the user's keystrokes\n*Explanation: Associating labels with inputs enables assistive technologies to identify fields and makes tapping the label focus the input.*\n\n**Question 2:** Which semantic HTML element represents the primary, unique content of a webpage?\n- A) `<div class=\"main-body\">`\n- B) `<main>` *(Correct)*\n- C) `<section>`\n- D) `<aside>`\n*Explanation: The <main> element specifies the unique, primary content of the document, excluding repeated headers, footers, and sidebars.*\n\n---\n\n### 7. Module Summary & Key Takeaways\n- Always write valid HTML5 with proper character encoding and responsive viewport metadata.\n- Use semantic landmarks (`<header>`, `<nav>`, `<main>`, `<footer>`) instead of generic `<div>` containers.\n- Form inputs must always have explicit, accessible `<label>` associations.\n- Provide descriptive `alt` text for all informational images to uphold WCAG accessibility.",
