        "number": 4,
        "title": "JavaScript From Zero",
        "subtitle": "ES6+ syntax, functions, DOM manipulation, asynchronous programming, and REST APIs",
        "readingTime": "30 min",
        "summary": "Master the programming language of the web: variables, arrays, objects, DOM manipulation, event listeners, Promises, async/await, and fetching remote data.",
        "content": "### Learning Objectives\nBy the end of this module, you will be able to:\n1. Declare variables, state, and constants with modern `const` and `let` scoping rules.\n2. Transform and query data collections using modern array methods (`map`, `filter`, `reduce`, `find`).\n3. Select and dynamically update DOM elements, handle events, and validate user input without page reloads.\n4. Manage asynchronous tasks using Promises and modern `async` / `await` syntax with `try / catch`.\n5. Fetch and render data from third-party REST APIs using the browser `fetch()` API.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Modern JavaScript Syntax (ES6+)\n\n```javascript\n// 1. Immutable vs Reassignable Variables\nconst platformName = \"FAHMRISE\";\nlet activeStudents = 1250;\nactiveStudents += 1;\n\n// 2. Objects & Destructuring\nconst student = {\n  id: \"std_101\",\n  name: \"Amina Kimani\",\n  track: \"Frontend Developer\",\n  skills: [\"HTML\", \"CSS\", \"JavaScript\"]\n};\n\nconst { name, track, skills } = student;\nconsole.log(`Student ${name} is enrolled in ${track}.`);\n\n// 3. Arrow Functions\nconst calculateTuition = (monthlyRate, months) => monthlyRate * months;\n```\n\n---\n\n### 2. High-Order Array Methods\n\nNever mutate raw arrays; use declarative methods:\n\n```javascript\nconst courses = [\n  { id: 1, title: \"Frontend Developer\", price: 3000, category: \"Technology\" },\n  { id: 2, title: \"Data Analysis\", price: 5000, category: \"Technology\" },\n  { id: 3, title: \"Digital Entrepreneurship\", price: 8000, category: \"Business\" }\n];\n\n// Filter: Get all Technology courses\nconst techCourses = courses.filter(c => c.category === \"Technology\");\n\n// Map: Extract formatted title strings\nconst courseLabels = courses.map(c => `${c.title} - KSh ${c.price}`);\n\n// Reduce: Calculate total catalog revenue potential\nconst totalRevenue = courses.reduce((sum, c) => sum + c.price, 0);\n```\n\n---\n\n### 3. DOM Manipulation & Event Handling\n\n```javascript\n// Select elements\nconst button = document.querySelector(\"#btn-enroll\");\nconst countDisplay = document.querySelector(\"#enrolled-count\");\n\nlet count = 0;\n\n// Attach Event Listener\nbutton.addEventListener(\"click\", () => {\n  count += 1;\n  countDisplay.textContent = `Total Enrolled: ${count}`;\n  button.classList.add(\"bg-emerald-600\");\n});\n```\n\n---\n\n### 4. Asynchronous JavaScript & REST APIs\n\nModern web applications fetch live data over the network:\n\n```javascript\nasync function loadCourseCatalog() {\n  try {\n    const response = await fetch(\"/api/books\");\n    \n    if (!response.ok) {\n      throw new Error(`HTTP error! Status: ${response.status}`);\n    }\n\n    const data = await response.json();\n    console.log(\"Loaded courses:\", data.books);\n    renderCoursesToDOM(data.books);\n  } catch (error) {\n    console.error(\"Failed to load catalog:\", error.message);\n    document.querySelector(\"#error-banner\").textContent = \"Could not load courses. Please retry.\";\n  }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n### 5. Activities & Practical Exercises\n\n#### Exercise 1: Dynamic Task List with LocalStorage\nCreate a small web app with an input field, an \"Add Task\" button, and an unordered list. When the user clicks \"Add\", append the task to the DOM and persist the array in `localStorage`. When the page reloads, retrieve and render the saved tasks.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Module Knowledge Quiz\n\n**Question 1:** What is the output of `[1, 2, 3, 4].filter(num => num % 2 === 0).map(num => num * 10)`?\n- A) `[10, 20, 30, 40]`\n- B) `[20, 40]` *(Correct)*\n- C) `[2, 4]`\n- D) `60`\n*Explanation: filter(num => num % 2 === 0) returns [2, 4], and map(num => num * 10) produces [20, 40].*\n\n**Question 2:** Why is using `async/await` with `try/catch` preferred for API requests?\n- A) It eliminates the need for internet connectivity\n- B) It makes asynchronous code look synchronous and provides clean, centralized error handling *(Correct)*\n- C) It converts JSON to XML automatically\n- D) It increases CPU clock speed\n*Explanation: async/await provides a readable, non-blocking way to handle Promises with intuitive try/catch error management.*\n\n---\n\n### 7. Module Summary & Key Takeaways\n- Use `const` by default, and `let` only when variable reassignment is strictly required.\n- Use immutable array methods (`map`, `filter`, `reduce`) to transform data collections.\n- Listen to user actions with `addEventListener` and update the DOM dynamically.\n- Use `async/await` and `fetch()` with robust `try/catch` blocks to consume REST APIs.",
