            "explanation": "### What is a Variable?\nA variable is a named storage location in memory for holding a value.\n\nIn modern JavaScript (ES6+), we declare variables using **`const`** and **`let`**:\n\n#### 1. `const` (Constant - Default Choice)\nUse `const` for values that should **never be reassigned**:\n```javascript\nconst courseName = \"JavaScript From Zero\";\nconst passingScore = 80;\n\n// Attempting to reassign will throw a TypeError:\n// courseName = \"Advanced JS\"; // Error!\n```\n\n#### 2. `let` (Reassignable Variable)\nUse `let` when you know the variable's value will change over time:\n```javascript\nlet currentScore = 0;\ncurrentScore = 15; // Reassignment allowed\ncurrentScore = currentScore + 10; // Now 25\n```\n\n### Golden Rule of Variable Declaration:\n> **Default to `const`**. Only change to `let` if you explicitly need to reassign the variable later.",
