            "explanation": "### The Core Primitives\nJavaScript has 7 primitive types. The three most common are:\n\n1. **String**: Text enclosed in quotes (`\"...\"`, `'...' `, or ``...``).\n```javascript\nconst name = \"Fatima\";\nconst city = 'Nairobi';\n```\n\n2. **Number**: Integers and floating-point decimal numbers (JavaScript uses 64-bit float for all numbers).\n```javascript\nconst age = 22;\nconst price = 99.95;\n```\n\n3. **Boolean**: Logical values representing `true` or `false`.\n```javascript\nconst isEnrolled = true;\nconst hasSubmitted = false;\n```\n\n### Checking Types with `typeof`\nYou can inspect the type of any value using the `typeof` operator:\n```javascript\nconsole.log(typeof \"FAHMRISE\"); // \"string\"\nconsole.log(typeof 42);         // \"number\"\nconsole.log(typeof true);       // \"boolean\"\n```",
