            "explanation": "### Comparison Operators\nComparison operators evaluate expressions to a Boolean (`true` or `false`):\n- `>` (Greater than), `<` (Less than)\n- `>=` (Greater than or equal), `<=` (Less than or equal)\n\n### Strict Equality (`===`) vs Loose Equality (`==`)\n\n#### 1. Strict Equality (`===`) - ALWAYS USE THIS\nChecks **both value and data type** without automatic type coercion:\n```javascript\n5 === 5;     // true\n5 === \"5\";   // false (number !== string)\n0 === false; // false\n```\n\n#### 2. Loose Equality (`==`) - NEVER USE THIS\nPerforms implicit type coercion before comparing, causing dangerous bugs:\n```javascript\n5 == \"5\";    // true (danger! string coerced to number)\n0 == false;  // true (danger!)\n\"\" == false; // true (danger!)\n```\n\n> **Rule**: Always use strict equality (`===`) and strict inequality (`!==`).",
            "keyPoints": [
              "Strict equality `===` checks both value and type without converting types.",
              "Loose equality `==` coerces types behind the scenes, leading to subtle logic errors.",
              "Use strict inequality `!==` instead of `!=`."
            ],
            "examples": [
              {
                "title": "Comparing inputs securely",
                "code": "const userInput = \"100\";\nconst targetScore = 100;\n\nconsole.log(userInput === targetScore); // false (string vs number)\nconsole.log(Number(userInput) === targetScore); // true (explicit conversion)",
                "description": "Explicit conversion with Number() combined with === makes intent crystal clear."
              }
            ],
            "practice": "Test comparisons like `10 === '10'` and `true === 1` in the console.",
