            "id": "js-17-1",
            "title": "Application Architecture & State Management Pattern",
            "subtitle": "Single source of truth and predictable data flow",
            "readingTime": "5 min",
            "explanation": "### What is Application State?\n**State** is the single source of truth describing what your application currently looks like and contains (e.g. current learner, active course, completed lessons, filter query).\n\n```\n       +------------------------------------+\n       |       Central App State            |\n       |  { user, activeLesson, notes: [] } |\n       +-----------------+------------------+\n                         |\n           +-------------+-------------+\n           |                           |\n           v                           v\n   [ User Action ]            [ Render Function ]\n   (Click, Input)             (Updates DOM from State)\n```\n\n### The State-Driven UI Pattern:\n1. State is stored in a central object.\n2. User interactions update the **State** (never modifying DOM directly in ad-hoc ways).\n3. The `render()` function updates the DOM to match the updated State.",
            "keyPoints": [
              "State represents the current snapshot of your application data.",
              "Keep state centralized rather than reading values out of the DOM.",
              "State changes trigger predictable UI re-renders."
            ],
            "examples": [
              {
                "title": "Central state object with subscriber",
                "code": "const state = {\n  filter: \"all\",\n  courses: []\n};\n\nfunction setFilter(newFilter) {\n  state.filter = newFilter;\n  renderApp(); // Re-renders UI based on updated state\n}",
                "description": "State update driving clean UI re-render."
              }
            ],
            "practice": "Design a state object for a task management application.",
            "challenge": {
              "type": "quiz",
              "question": "In the State-Driven UI pattern, where is the single source of truth for the application data kept?",
              "options": [
                "Directly inside the HTML `data-` attributes of every button.",
                "In a centralized JavaScript state object.",
                "In the URL query parameter string only.",
                "In temporary CSS classes."
              ],
              "correctAnswer": 1,
              "explanation": "In modern application architecture, a centralized state object holds the authoritative single source of truth."
            }
