        "title": "Organising and Validating Data",
        "subtitle": "Structured tables, multi-level sorting, custom filtering, data validation dropdowns, duplicate removal, and clean data entry",
        "readingTime": "20 min",
        "summary": "Master the principles of tidy data, convert ranges to structured tables, configure multi-level sorting and custom filters, build input-restricting Data Validation rules, and remove duplicate records.",
        "content": "### Learning Objectives\nBy the end of this module, you will be able to:\n1. Apply the 4 Rules of Tidy Tabular Data to design clean, analysis-ready spreadsheets.\n2. Convert raw cell ranges into official Structured Tables (`Ctrl + T` in Excel) and utilize structured referencing.\n3. Execute multi-level sorts (e.g. County ascending, then Total Revenue descending).\n4. Apply custom text, numeric, and date filters to isolate target operational segments.\n5. Configure Data Validation dropdown lists, number ranges, and custom error alerts to eliminate typos.\n6. Detect and remove duplicate records safely without losing transaction history.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The 4 Rules of Tidy Tabular Data\n\nBefore writing a single formula or creating a PivotTable, your dataset must be structured as a **tidy table**:\n\n1. **Rule 1: One Header Row**: The top row contains clear, distinct, single-line column headers. Never use merged cells in header rows!\n2. **Rule 2: One Record Per Row**: Each row represents a single unique transaction, customer, or observation.\n3. **Rule 3: One Attribute Per Column**: Each column contains only one category of data (e.g. separate `First Name` and `Last Name`, never combine them with phone numbers in one cell).\n4. **Rule 4: No Blank Header Cells or Merged Data Ranges**: Merged cells destroy column filtering, formula references, and PivotTable creation.\n\n```\nBAD (Merged & Messy):                   GOOD (Tidy & Structured Table):\n+-----------------------------+         +-----------+------------+------------+\n|        JANUARY SALES        | (Merged)+ Date      | County     | Sales (KSh)|\n+---------+---------+---------+         +-----------+------------+------------+\n| Nairobi | Mombasa | Kisumu  |         | 2026-01-05| Nairobi    | 45000      |\n+---------+---------+---------+         | 2026-01-05| Mombasa    | 32000      |\n| 45000   | 32000   | 18000   |         | 2026-01-05| Kisumu     | 18000      |\n+---------+---------+---------+         +-----------+------------+------------+\n```\n\n---\n\n### 2. Structured Tables (Ctrl + T)\n\nConverting a raw range to an official Structured Table (`Insert -> Table` or shortcut `Ctrl + T`) provides significant analytical advantages:\n- **Dynamic Range Expansion**: Adding new rows automatically expands the table and all connected formulas/PivotTables.\n- **Calculated Columns**: Entering a formula in one cell automatically populates the entire column.\n- **Structured References**: Formulas use readable column names (`=[@UnitPrice]*[@Quantity]`) instead of cryptic coordinates (`=E2*F2`).\n- **Total Row Toggle**: Check \"Total Row\" in Table Design to instantly calculate Sum, Average, or Count with a single click.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Multi-Level Sorting & Custom Filtering\n\nReal-world datasets require multi-dimensional sorting:\n1. **Multi-Level Sort**:\n   - Go to `Data -> Sort`.\n   - Level 1: Sort by `Region` (A to Z).\n   - Level 2: Click \"Add Level\", sort by `Total Revenue` (Largest to Smallest).\n2. **Filtering Capabilities**:\n   - **Text Filters**: \"Begins with\", \"Contains\", \"Custom filter\".\n   - **Number Filters**: \"Greater than\", \"Between\", \"Top 10 items\", \"Above Average\".\n   - **Date Filters**: \"Last Month\", \"This Quarter\", \"Year to Date\".\n\n---\n\n### 4. Data Validation: Error-Proofing Data Entry\n\nTypos corrupt analytical results. If one salesperson types `\"Nairobi\"`, another types `\"nbi\"`, and another types `\"Nairobi \"` (with trailing space), formulas will treat them as three completely different cities!\n\n#### How to Configure Data Validation Dropdowns:\n1. Select the target column/range (e.g. `C2:C100`).\n2. Navigate to `Data -> Data Validation`.\n3. In **Allow**, choose **List**.\n4. In **Source**, type comma-separated values: `Nairobi, Mombasa, Kisumu, Nakuru, Eldoret` (or select a reference range `=$K$2:$K$6`).\n5. Configure **Error Alert**: Set Style to **Stop**, Title: *\"Invalid County\"*, Message: *\"Please select a valid Kenyan county from the dropdown list.\"*\n\n---\n\n### 5. Kenyan Business Dataset: Mombasa Secondary School Student Enrolment\n\n| Student ID | Full Name | Gender | Stream | Term 1 Fee (KSh) | Fee Paid (KSh) | Status | Registered Date |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **STU-201** | Amina Omar | Female | Form 3 East | 35,000.00 | 35,000.00 | Cleared | 2026-01-08 |\n| **STU-202** | Brian Kiprono | Male | Form 3 North| 35,000.00 | 20,000.00 | Partial | 2026-01-09 |\n| **STU-203** | Faith Wanjiku | Female | Form 3 East | 35,000.00 | 35,000.00 | Cleared | 2026-01-10 |\n| **STU-204** | Dennis Mutua | Male | Form 3 West | 35,000.00 | 10,000.00 | Partial | 2026-01-11 |\n| **STU-205** | Halima Hassan| Female | Form 3 North| 35,000.00 | 0.00 | Pending | 2026-01-12 |\n\n---\n\n### 6. Removing Duplicate Records\n\nAccidental duplicate entries distort revenue and inventory calculations:\n1. Select your table range.\n2. Navigate to `Data -> Remove Duplicates`.\n3. Check the primary unique identifier column (e.g. `Student ID` or `Invoice ID`).\n4. Click **OK**. The spreadsheet will report how many duplicate rows were removed and how many unique rows remain.\n\n---\n\n### 7. Guided Hands-On Exercise\n1. Enter the student enrolment data into a sheet named `Enrolment_2026`.\n2. Convert the range into a Structured Table using `Ctrl + T`.\n3. Apply a Data Validation dropdown on the `Status` column restricting input to: `Cleared, Partial, Pending`.\n4. Apply a filter to display only students whose `Status` is `Partial` or `Pending`.\n5. Add a calculated column named `Balance Due` with formula: `=[@[Term 1 Fee (KSh)]]-[@[Fee Paid (KSh)]]`.\n6. Verify total outstanding balance for partial/pending students equals **KSh 75,000.00**.\n\n---\n\n### 8. Module Summary & Key Takeaways\n- Tidy tables follow strict structural rules: one header row, one record per row, and no merged cells.\n- Structured Tables (`Ctrl + T`) automatically expand formulas, formatting, and references.\n- Data Validation dropdowns eliminate misspelled categories at the point of entry.\n- Remove Duplicates cleans transaction databases before aggregations are executed.",
        "challenge": {
          "type": "quiz",
          "title": "Module 2 Checkpoint: Organising and Validating Data",
          "question": "Why should you avoid merging cells across data rows (e.g. merging cells A2 and A3) in an analysis spreadsheet?",
          "options": [
            "Merged cells make file sizes 10 times larger",
            "Merged cells break column sorting, prevent filtering, and cause PivotTables to fail",
            "Spreadsheets crash immediately when saving merged cells",
            "Merged cells can only store Boolean values"
          ],
          "correctAnswer": 1,
          "explanation": "Merged cells prevent the spreadsheet engine from recognizing individual row/column records. As a result, sorting shifts data into the wrong rows, filters fail to hide target records, and PivotTables cannot index the data structure."
        }
