        "title": "Essential Spreadsheet Formulas and Functions",
        "subtitle": "Formula anatomy, cell references ($), SUM, AVERAGE, COUNT, COUNTA, MIN, MAX, IF logic, SUMIF, COUNTIF, and error handling",
        "readingTime": "22 min",
        "summary": "Master the mathematical and logical engine of spreadsheets: relative ($A1) and absolute ($A$1) references, foundational aggregations, COUNT vs COUNTA, conditional IF branching, SUMIF/COUNTIF, and error diagnostics.",
        "content": "### Learning Objectives\nBy the end of this module, you will be able to:\n1. Construct spreadsheet formulas with standard operators (`+`, `-`, `*`, `/`, `^`, `&`, `>`, `<`).\n2. Master relative (`A1`), absolute (`$A$1`), and mixed (`$A1`, `A$1`) referencing using the `F4` shortcut key.\n3. Compute summary statistics using `SUM`, `AVERAGE`, `MIN`, `MAX`, `COUNT`, and `COUNTA`.\n4. Implement conditional logic with single and nested `IF` statements.\n5. Aggregate specific subsets using single-criterion `SUMIF`, `COUNTIF`, and `AVERAGEIF`.\n6. Diagnose and fix common formula errors (`#DIV/0!`, `#VALUE!`, `#REF!`, `#NAME?`, `#N/A`).\n\n---\n\n### 1. Anatomy of a Spreadsheet Formula\n\nEvery formula begins with an equals sign (`=`).\n- Syntax: `=FUNCTION_NAME(argument1, argument2, ...)`\n- Mathematical Operators: `+` (Add), `-` (Subtract), `*` (Multiply), `/` (Divide), `^` (Exponent).\n- Comparison Operators: `=` (Equal to), `<>` (Not equal to), `>` (Greater than), `>=` (Greater than or equal to), `<` (Less than), `<=` (Less than or equal to).\n\n---\n\n### 2. Relative vs. Absolute Cell Referencing ($)\n\nThe dollar sign (`$`) locks row or column coordinates when copying formulas across cells:\n\n| Reference Type | Example | When Copied to Another Cell... | Common Use Case |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Relative** | `A1` | Both column and row change dynamically | Multiplying `=Price * Qty` along a row |\n| **Absolute** | `$A$1` | Both column and row stay strictly locked | Multiplying by fixed VAT rate in cell `$B$1` |\n| **Mixed (Column Locked)** | `$A1` | Column A stays locked; row changes | Multi-column comparison tables |\n| **Mixed (Row Locked)** | `A$1` | Column changes; Row 1 stays locked | Horizontal monthly header lookups |\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Keyboard Shortcut**: Press **`F4`** (or **`Fn + F4`**) while editing a cell reference to toggle instantly between: `A1` -> `$A$1` -> `A$1` -> `$A1` -> `A1`.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Core Aggregation & Counting Functions\n\n- **`=SUM(B2:B10)`**: Adds all numeric values in range.\n- **`=AVERAGE(B2:B10)`**: Calculates arithmetic mean (ignores blank cells and text strings).\n- **`=MIN(B2:B10)`** / **`=MAX(B2:B10)`**: Identifies smallest and largest numbers.\n- **`=COUNT(A2:A10)`**: Counts cells containing **numeric values only**.\n- **`=COUNTA(A2:A10)`**: Counts all **non-empty cells** (numbers, text, codes, dates).\n- **`=COUNTBLANK(A2:A10)`**: Counts empty cells in range.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Logical Decisions with IF\n\nThe `IF` function evaluates a condition and returns one value if TRUE, and another if FALSE:\n\n```excel\n=IF(logical_test, value_if_true, value_if_false)\n```\n\n#### Example 1: Sales Commission Bonus\nIf sales in cell `B2` exceed KSh 100,000, grant a 5% bonus; otherwise, 0:\n```excel\n=IF(B2 > 100000, B2 * 0.05, 0)\n```\n\n#### Example 2: Nested IF for Performance Grading\n```excel\n=IF(B2 >= 80, \"Distinction\", IF(B2 >= 60, \"Credit\", IF(B2 >= 40, \"Pass\", \"Referral\")))\n```\n\n---\n\n### 5. Conditional Aggregations: SUMIF & COUNTIF\n\nAggregate only those rows that meet a specific business condition:\n\n1. **`SUMIF` Syntax**: `=SUMIF(range_to_evaluate, criteria, [sum_range])`\n   - Calculate total sales for \"Mombasa\" branch:\n     ```excel\n     =SUMIF(B2:B50, \"Mombasa\", E2:E50)\n     ```\n   - Sum transactions greater than KSh 50,000:\n     ```excel\n     =SUMIF(E2:E50, \">50000\")\n     ```\n\n2. **`COUNTIF` Syntax**: `=COUNTIF(range, criteria)`\n   - Count how many sales were made via M-Pesa:\n     ```excel\n     =COUNTIF(F2:F50, \"M-Pesa\")\n     ```\n\n3. **`AVERAGEIF` Syntax**: `=AVERAGEIF(range_to_evaluate, criteria, [average_range])`\n   - Average order value for \"Electronics\":\n     ```excel\n     =AVERAGEIF(C2:C50, \"Electronics\", E2:E50)\n     ```\n\n---\n\n### 6. Kenyan Business Dataset: Eldoret Farm Fresh Produce\n\n| Batch ID | Crop Name | Farm County | Bags Sold | Price per Bag (KSh) | Gross Sales (KSh) | Quality Grade |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **BCH-01** | Maize | Uasin Gishu | 120 | 3,800.00 | 456,000.00 | Grade A |\n| **BCH-02** | Wheat | Trans Nzoia | 85 | 4,200.00 | 357,000.00 | Grade A |\n| **BCH-03** | Potatoes | Nyandarua | 210 | 2,100.00 | 441,000.00 | Grade B |\n| **BCH-04** | Maize | Trans Nzoia | 160 | 3,600.00 | 576,000.00 | Grade B |\n| **BCH-05** | Beans | Uasin Gishu | 65 | 9,500.00 | 617,500.00 | Grade A |\n\n---\n\n### 7. Deconstructing Common Error Codes\n\n- **`#DIV/0!`**: Formula attempted to divide by zero or an empty cell.\n- **`#VALUE!`**: Wrong argument type (e.g. attempting to multiply text by a number).\n- **`#REF!`**: Cell reference is invalid (usually caused by deleting a row/column used in a formula).\n- **`#NAME?`**: Misspelled function name (e.g. `=SUMM(A1:A5)`).\n- **`#N/A`**: \"No value available\" (lookup function could not find the target key).\n\n---\n\n### 8. Guided Hands-On Exercise\n1. Enter the Eldoret Farm dataset in a sheet named `Agri_Sales`.\n2. In column F, compute Gross Sales: `=D2*E2`.\n3. In cell `I2`, calculate Total Maize Sales using `SUMIF`: `=SUMIF(B2:B6, \"Maize\", F2:F6)` -> **Expected: KSh 1,032,000.00**.\n4. In cell `I3`, count how many batches are \"Grade A\" using `COUNTIF`: `=COUNTIF(G2:G6, \"Grade A\")` -> **Expected: 3**.\n5. In cell `I4`, compute Average Batch Sales for Uasin Gishu: `=AVERAGEIF(C2:C6, \"Uasin Gishu\", F2:F6)` -> **Expected: KSh 536,750.00**.\n\n---\n\n### 9. Module Summary & Key Takeaways\n- Cell referencing with `$` controls coordinate locking during formula propagation.\n- `SUM`, `AVERAGE`, `MIN`, `MAX`, and `COUNT`/`COUNTA` form the bedrock of numerical analysis.\n- `IF` handles branching business logic based on dynamic threshold tests.\n- `SUMIF` and `COUNTIF` allow conditional subset aggregations without manual filtering.",
