        "readingTime": "22 min",
        "summary": "Master the core Python data structures essential for data wrangling. Understand lists, dictionaries, sets, tuples, conditional branching with if/elif/else, and iterating through datasets with for loops.",
        "content": "### Learning Objectives\nBy the end of this module, you will be able to:\n1. Differentiate between Python's 4 primary collection types: **Lists**, **Dictionaries**, **Sets**, and **Tuples**.\n2. Perform slicing, indexing, appending, and mutating operations on lists.\n3. Access, update, and iterate over key-value pairs in **Dictionaries** (the foundation of JSON and DataFrame records).\n4. Remove duplicates and perform membership tests in (O(1)) time using **Sets**.\n5. Write robust algorithmic control flow using **`if / elif / else`** and **`for` loops** with **`enumerate()`** and **`zip()`**.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The 4 Essential Python Data Structures\n\n```python\n# 1. LIST: Ordered, mutable, allows duplicate items\nnairobi_branches = [\"CBD\", \"Westlands\", \"Kilimani\", \"Eastleigh\", \"CBD\"]\n\n# 2. SET: Unordered, mutable, unique elements only (great for deduplication!)\nunique_regions = set(nairobi_branches)  # {\"CBD\", \"Westlands\", \"Kilimani\", \"Eastleigh\"}\n\n# 3. TUPLE: Ordered, immutable (fixed coordinates / configurations)\ngps_coordinates = (-1.286389, 36.817223)  # Latitude, Longitude of Nairobi\n\n# 4. DICTIONARY: Key-value mapping (represents a structured data record)\ntransaction_record = {\n    \"transaction_id\": \"MP-98412\",\n    \"customer\": \"Amina Wanjiru\",\n    \"amount_ksh\": 14500.0,\n    \"payment_channel\": \"M-Pesa\",\n    \"is_verified\": True\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n### 2. Iterating with enumerate() and zip()\n\n```python\nmonths = [\"January\", \"February\", \"March\"]\nrevenues_ksh = [450000, 520000, 610000]\n\n# zip() pairs two iterables element-by-element\nfor month, rev in zip(months, revenues_ksh):\n    print(f\"Month: {month} -> Revenue: KSh {rev:,}\")\n\n# enumerate() provides automatic index counters\nfor rank, branch in enumerate(nairobi_branches[:3], start=1):\n    print(f\"Rank #{rank}: {branch}\")\n```\n\n---\n\n### 3. Summary & Best Practices\n- Use Dictionaries when modeling structured record rows with named fields.\n- Use Sets for lightning-fast (O(1)) membership testing (`if customer_id in active_set`).",
