        "summary": "Write clean, modular, and fault-tolerant data transformation code. Master list and dictionary comprehensions, author reusable cleaning functions, apply lambda functions, and handle data errors gracefully with try/except.",
        "content": "### Learning Objectives\nBy the end of this module, you will be able to:\n1. Define modular Python functions with docstrings, default arguments, and return values.\n2. Refactor verbose for-loops into concise, high-speed **List and Dictionary Comprehensions**.\n3. Author inline **Lambda Functions** for lightweight column transformations.\n4. Protect data ingestion pipelines against unexpected corrupt records using **`try / except / finally`** blocks.\n5. Parse and clean raw dirty Kenyan currency strings (`\"KSh 14,500.00\"` -> `14500.0`).\n\n---\n\n### 1. List and Dictionary Comprehensions\n\nComprehensions provide a clean, idiomatic syntax for mapping and filtering collections:\n\n```python\nraw_sales = [1200, 4500, 8900, 3200, 15000]\n\n# List Comprehension: Apply 16% VAT tax only to items above KSh 3,000\ntaxed_sales = [round(amt * 1.16, 2) for amt in raw_sales if amt > 3000]\nprint(taxed_sales)  # [5220.0, 10324.0, 3712.0, 17400.0]\n\n# Dictionary Comprehension: Convert exchange rates\nusd_prices = {\"Laptop\": 800, \"Monitor\": 250, \"Mouse\": 25}\nUSD_TO_KSH = 132.50\nksh_prices = {item: price * USD_TO_KSH for item, price in usd_prices.items()}\n```\n\n---\n\n### 2. Robust Data Cleaning Function with try/except\n\n```python\ndef clean_currency_string(raw_val: str) -> float:\n    \"\"\"\n    Sanitizes raw Kenyan currency strings into clean float values.\n    Examples:\n        'KSh 14,500.00' -> 14500.0\n        ' 2,300 '       -> 2300.0\n        'N/A' or None   -> 0.0\n    \"\"\"\n    if raw_val is None:\n        return 0.0\n    try:\n        cleaned = str(raw_val).replace(\"KSh\", \"\").replace(\",\", \"\").strip()\n        return float(cleaned)\n    except (ValueError, TypeError) as err:\n        # Gracefully handle non-numeric corrupt entries\n        return 0.0\n\n# Testing:\nprint(clean_currency_string(\"KSh 45,999.50\"))  # 45999.5\nprint(clean_currency_string(\"Invalid Text\"))   # 0.0 (No crash!)\n```\n\n---\n\n### 3. Summary & Best Practices\n- Always wrap fragile data parsing logic (dates, currencies, JSON) in `try/except` blocks to prevent pipeline aborts.\n- Use list comprehensions when creating new lists from existing collections.",
