        "title": "Data Cleaning and Transformation with pandas",
        "subtitle": "Handling missing values (isna, fillna, dropna), type coercion, string methods (.str), and date parsing",
        "readingTime": "24 min",
        "summary": "Sanitize real-world messy commercial datasets. Master missing value imputation, string manipulation with .str accessors, parsing irregular datetime columns with pd.to_datetime, and detecting statistical outliers.",
        "content": "### Learning Objectives\nBy the end of this module, you will be able to:\n1. Detect, count, and visualize missing values using **`df.isna().sum()`**.\n2. Apply appropriate imputation strategies: **`fillna()`**, **`dropna()`**, forward-fill (`ffill`), and mean/median substitution.\n3. Vectorize string cleanup using **`.str` accessors** (`.str.strip()`, `.str.lower()`, `.str.replace()`, `.str.extract()`).\n4. Convert date strings to rich `datetime64` objects with **`pd.to_datetime()`** and extract calendar attributes via **`.dt` accessors**.\n5. Identify and remove duplicate records using **`df.duplicated()`** and **`df.drop_duplicates()`**.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Missing Value Strategy: Detect, Drop, or Impute?\n\n```python\n# 1. Audit missingness across all columns\nmissing_audit = df.isna().sum()\nmissing_pct = (df.isna().mean() * 100).round(2)\nprint(pd.DataFrame({\"missing_count\": missing_audit, \"missing_percent\": missing_pct}))\n\n# 2. Strategy A: Drop rows where critical primary identifiers are missing\ndf_clean = df.dropna(subset=[\"transaction_id\", \"customer_id\"])\n\n# 3. Strategy B: Impute missing numerical costs with category median\ndf_clean[\"unit_cost_ksh\"] = df_clean.groupby(\"category\")[\"unit_cost_ksh\"].transform(\n    lambda x: x.fillna(x.median())\n)\n\n# 4. Strategy C: Impute categorical missing values with 'Unknown'\ndf_clean[\"payment_method\"] = df_clean[\"payment_method\"].fillna(\"Unspecified\")\n```\n\n---\n\n### 2. Vectorized String & Datetime Cleaning\n\n```python\n# Vectorized String Cleanup\ndf[\"clean_customer\"] = df[\"raw_name\"].str.strip().str.title()\ndf[\"clean_phone\"] = df[\"raw_phone\"].str.replace(r\"[^d]\", \"\", regex=True)\n\n# Datetime Conversion & Attribute Extraction\ndf[\"order_timestamp\"] = pd.to_datetime(df[\"order_date_str\"], format=\"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S\", errors=\"coerce\")\ndf[\"order_year\"] = df[\"order_timestamp\"].dt.year\ndf[\"order_month_name\"] = df[\"order_timestamp\"].dt.month_name()\ndf[\"order_day_of_week\"] = df[\"order_timestamp\"].dt.day_name()\ndf[\"order_hour\"] = df[\"order_timestamp\"].dt.hour\n```\n\n---\n\n### 3. Summary & Best Practices\n- Never use mean imputation when a distribution contains severe outliers; use **median imputation**.\n- Use `pd.to_datetime(errors='coerce')` to convert unparseable corrupt date strings into `NaT` safely.",
