            "sound": "Heavy elevation (Isti'la), palatal compression (Itbaq), and fluid speed (Idhlaq)",
            "explanation": "The remaining three opposite pairs govern the **Heaviness (Elevation)**, **Palatal Compression (Adhesion)**, and **Acoustic Fluency** of Arabic letters.\n\n### 3. Elevation of the Tongue: Al-Isti‘lā' vs Al-Istifāl\n* **Al-Isti‘lā' (الِاسْتِعْلَاء = Elevation / Heaviness / Tafkhīm)**: Raising the back root of the tongue toward the roof of the mouth (soft palate). This redirects the acoustic energy upward, creating a deep, echoing, full-mouthed resonance (Tafkhīm).\n  - **The 7 Isti'lā' Letters**: Memorized in the mnemonic:\n  - **« خُصَّ ضَغْطٍ قِظْ »** (Khā' خ, Ṣād ص, Ḍād ض, Ghayn غ, Ṭā' ط, Qāf ق, Ẓā' ظ).\n  - *Rule*: These 7 letters are **always heavy** in every state (whether with Fatḥah, Ḍammah, Kasrah, or Sukūn).\n* **Al-Istifāl (الِاسْتِفَال = Lowering / Flatness / Tarqīq)**: Lowering the tongue to the floor of the mouth. The sound escapes outward without echoing in the roof, producing a light, smiling resonance (Tarqīq). The remaining 21 letters are Istifāl letters.\n\n### 4. Palatal Adhesion: Al-Iṭbāq vs Al-Infitāḥ\n* **Al-Iṭbāq (الْإِطْبَاق = Adhesion / Sound Compression)**: In addition to elevating the back of the tongue, the center/body of the tongue arches and seals closely against the roof of the mouth, trapping and compressing the sound inside the mouth dome.\n  - **The 4 Iṭbāq Letters**: **« ص , ض , ط , ظ »** (Ṣād, Ḍād, Ṭā', Ẓā').\n  - *Hierarchy of Heaviness*: **Ṭā' (ط)** > **Ḍād (ض)** > **Ṣād (ص)** > **Ẓā' (ظ)**.\n  - *Golden Rule*: Every Iṭbāq letter is automatically an Isti'lā' letter, but NOT every Isti'lā' letter has Iṭbāq. The 4 Iṭbāq letters are significantly heavier and more compressed than `ق, غ, خ`.\n* **Al-Infitāḥ (الِانْفِتَاح = Openness)**: The body of the tongue does not adhere to the roof of the mouth; open space allows the acoustic sound to disperse freely (the remaining 24 letters).\n\n### 5. Articulation Fluency: Al-Idhlāq vs Al-Iṣmāt\n* **Al-Idhlāq (الْإِذْلَاق = Fluency / Swiftness)**: Letters articulated swiftly and easily from the tip (Dhalq) of the tongue or the lips.\n  - **The 6 Idhlāq Letters**: **« فَرَّ مِنْ لُبٍّ »** (Fā' ف, Rā' ر, Mīm م, Nūn ن, Lām ل, Bā' ب).\n* **Al-Iṣmāt (الْإِصْمَات = Restraint / Solidity)**: Letters articulated with deliberate stability away from the extreme tips. In classical Arabic morphology, no authentic 4- or 5-letter root word consists purely of Iṣmāt letters without at least one Idhlāq letter.",
