        "content": "\n# Module 3: Conditions (Shuroot) & Essential Pillars (Arkan) of Salah\n\nTo ensure your prayer is sound, Islamic jurists categorize the components of Salah into clear legal classifications.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Conditions of Prayer (*Shuroot as-Salah*)\n\n**Conditions (*Shuroot*)** are prerequisites that must be fulfilled **before entering the prayer** and must remain continuous throughout:\n\n1. **Islam**: Being a Muslim.\n2. **Sanity (*'Aql*)**: Being conscious and of sound mind.\n3. **Discernment (*Tamyeez*)**: Reaching the age of discernment (around 7 years).\n4. **Purity from Impurity (*Taharah*)**: Having Wudu/Ghusl and clean body/clothes/area.\n5. **Entry of the Prayer Time (*Dukhul al-Waqt*)**: Praying within the prescribed window.\n6. **Covering the Awrah (*Satr al-'Awrah*)**:\n   * *For Men*: From the navel to the knee at minimum, with shoulders covered during prayer.\n   * *For Women*: The entire body except the face and hands (loose, non-transparent clothing).\n7. **Facing the Qiblah (*Istiqbal al-Qiblah*)**: Facing the direction of the Holy Ka'bah in Makkah.\n8. **Intention (*Niyyah*)**: Having the internal resolve in the heart to pray that specific prayer for Allah.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Pillars of Prayer (*Arkan as-Salah*)\n\n**Pillars (*Arkan*)** are the core, integral components of the prayer itself. If a pillar is omitted—whether intentionally or accidentally—the prayer is **invalid** and cannot be repaired by Sujood as-Sahw alone; the omitted pillar must be performed:\n\n1. **Standing if able (*Al-Qiyam*)** in obligatory prayers.\n2. **The Opening Takbir (*Takbirat al-Ihram*)** saying *Allahu Akbar*.\n3. **Reciting Surah Al-Fatihah** in every single Rak'ah.\n4. **Bowing (*Ar-Ruku'*)**.\n5. **Rising and standing straight from Ruku' (*Al-I'tidal*)**.\n6. **Prostrating on seven body parts (*As-Sujood*)**.\n7. **Rising from prostration and sitting between the two prostrations (*Al-Jalsah*)**.\n8. **Tranquility and stillness in all positions (*At-Tuma'neenah*)** (pausing long enough for bones to settle).\n9. **The Final Tashahhud sitting (*Al-Julus lit-Tashahhud al-Akhir*)**.\n10. **Reciting the Final Tashahhud**.\n11. **Sending Salawat upon the Prophet ﷺ in the final sitting**.\n12. **The Concluding Tasleem** (*Assalamu 'alaykum wa Rahmatullah*).\n13. **Observing the proper sequence (*At-Tarteeb*)** between all pillars.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Obligations (*Wajibah*) vs. Sunan\n\n* **Obligations (*Wajibah*)**: Acts like saying *Subhana Rabbiyal 'Adheem* in Ruku' or the first Tashahhud in 3- and 4-rak'ah prayers. If omitted intentionally, prayer is invalid; if omitted accidentally, it is repaired by **Sujood as-Sahw** (Prostration of Forgetfulness).\n* **Recommended Sunan (*Sunan as-Salah*)**: Raising hands with Takbir, looking at the place of prostration, reciting a surah after Al-Fatihah, post-prayer du'as. Omitting a Sunnah does not invalidate the prayer.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Respecting Classical Differences Across the Four Madhahib\n\nAll four recognized Sunni schools of jurisprudence (**Hanafi, Maliki, Shafi'i, Hanbali**) derive their rulings from the authentic Qur'an and Sunnah. Differences in minor details—such as hand placement (on the chest, above the navel, or by the side in the Maliki tradition), saying *Ameen* silently or aloud, or finger movement in Tashahhud—are **valid, respected juristic interpretations**.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> A beginner should avoid arguing over minor posture differences. The core pillars (standing, Fatihah, Ruku', Sujood, stillness, and intention) are agreed upon by all scholars.\n      ",
