        "content": "# Chapter 1: French Alphabet, Phonetics & Accents\n\nWelcome to **French Language Learning** on FAHMRISE! French is a beautiful, musical language renowned for its elegant rhythm and precise phonetics.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The French Alphabet & Key Letters\n\n| Letter | French Name | Phonetic / Notes |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **A** | /ɑ/ | Open 'ah' sound as in *father* |\n| **E** | /ə/ | Soft schwa sound as in *le* |\n| **G** | /ʒe/ | Soft 'zh' sound before e/i; hard 'g' before a/o/u |\n| **H** | /aʃ/ | **Always silent** in French (*l'homme*, *l'hôtel*) |\n| **J** | /ʒi/ | Soft 'zh' sound as in *measure* |\n| **R** | /ɛʁ/ | Guttural uvular sound produced in the back of the throat |\n| **U** | /y/ | Round lips into an 'O' shape and say 'EE' |\n\n---\n\n## 2. The 5 French Accent Marks\n\n1. **Accent Aigu (é):** Appears only on 'E' (*été, café, étudiant*). Produces a clear /e/ sound.\n2. **Accent Grave (à, è, ù):** Opens the vowel sound (*père, mère, à, où*).\n3. **Accent Circonflexe (â, ê, î, ô, û):** Indicates a historically dropped 's' (*fête, hôtel, château*).\n4. **Cédille (ç):** Turns a hard 'c' into a soft 's' sound before a, o, u (*français, garçon, leçon*).\n5. **Tréma (ë, ï):** Separates two consecutive vowels into distinct syllables (*Noël, maïs*).",
