        "transcript": "Karibu! Welcome to Chapter 1 of Swahili Language Learning. Kiswahili is a phonetic language, which means words are pronounced exactly as they are written. Every vowel is pure: A (/a/ as in 'father'), E (/e/ as in 'bed'), I (/i/ as in 'see'), O (/o/ as in 'go'), U (/u/ as in 'flute'). The most important pronunciation rule in Kiswahili is that the stress ALWAYS falls on the penultimate (second-to-last) syllable. For example, 'Kiswahili' is pronounced 'Ki-swa-HI-li', and 'Karibu' is 'ka-RI-bu'.",
