        "content": "# Chapter 1: Kiswahili Alphabet, Sounds & Syllable Stress\n\nKaribu sana! Welcome to **Swahili Language Learning** on FAHMRISE. Kiswahili is one of the most widely spoken languages in Africa and an official language of the African Union, East African Community, Kenya, and Tanzania.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The 5 Pure Vowels of Kiswahili\n\nUnlike English vowels which often glide, Kiswahili vowels are always pure, constant, and clear:\n\n| Vowel | IPA Sound | English Approximation | Kiswahili Example | Meaning |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **A** | /a/ | Like *a* in *father* | **Baba** (/ba.ba/) | Father |\n| **E** | /e/ | Like *e* in *bed* | **Mwalimu** (/mwa.li.mu/) | Teacher |\n| **I** | /i/ | Like *ee* in *see* | **Mji** (/m.ji/) | Town / City |\n| **O** | /o/ | Like *o* in *for* | **Moyo** (/mo.yo/) | Heart |\n| **U** | /u/ | Like *oo* in *food* | **Mtu** (/m.tu/) | Person |\n\n---\n\n## 2. The Golden Stress Rule: Penultimate Syllable\n\nIn Kiswahili, the primary vocal stress is **always** placed on the second-to-last syllable:\n- **Ki-swa-HI-li**\n- **ha-BA-ri**\n- **a-SAN-te**\n- **ta-FA-dha-li**",
