        "transcript": "In Chapter 4, we unlock the brilliant grammatical machinery of Kiswahili. Kiswahili is an agglutinative language: complete sentences are formed by assembling building blocks onto a verb root. A single word like 'Ninakupenda' contains: Ni- (Subject: I) + -na- (Tense: Present) + -ku- (Object: you) + -penda (Root: love) = 'I love you'! We will learn the primary subject prefixes (Ni, U, A, Tu, M, Wa), the 4 major tense markers (-NA-, -LI-, -TA-, -ME-), and the core noun classes (A-WA for people, KI-VI for objects).",
