        "content": "# Chapter 5: Numbers, Days, Dates, & Time\n\nLearn how to count, tell time, and schedule appointments in Somali.\n\n---\n\n## Numbers from 1 to 10 (Tirada)\n\n- **1** — Kow (Hal)\n- **2** — Laba\n- **3** — Saddex\n- **4** — Afar\n- **5** — Shan\n- **6** — Lix\n- **7** — Todoba\n- **8** — Sideed\n- **9** — Sagaal\n- **10** — Toban\n\n---\n\n## Days of the Week (Maalmaha Todobaadka)\n\n| Day | Somali Name | Notes |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Monday** | **Isniin** | Start of working week in many places |\n| **Tuesday** | **Talaado** | Third day |\n| **Wednesday** | **Arbaco** | Mid-week |\n| **Thursday** | **Khamiis** | Evening leading to Friday prayer |\n| **Friday** | **Jimce** | Sacred day of congregational prayer |\n| **Saturday** | **Sabti** | Weekend day |\n| **Sunday** | **Axad** | First day of traditional week |\n\n---\n\n## Telling Time (Saacadda)\n\n- **Saacaddu waa imisa?** — What time is it?\n- **Waa kowdii** — It is 1:00\n- **Waa labadii** — It is 2:00\n- **Waa lixdii iyo badh** — It is 6:30 (half past six)\n- **Maanta** — Today | **Shalay** — Yesterday | **Berri** — Tomorrow",
